  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a compressed-air energy storage concept, likely in an offshore setting. Electricity from a wind turbine is used to power a compressor, and the compressed air is injected underground into a subsurface storage formation beneath the seafloor or coastal area. A pipeline links this buried reservoir to an onshore facility, where the stored air is later brought back up, expanded in a decompression stage, and used to drive a generator connected to the power grid. Overall, the image shows how intermittent renewable electricity can be stored as pressurized air and recovered later for power generation.\n\nThe smaller panel on the right gives a pore-scale view of the storage process. It shows injected air moving through the spaces between solid grains and displacing or coexisting with the native pore fluid. Arrows indicate flow pathways of both the incoming air and the pore fluid around the grains. This inset helps explain the underground mechanism behind the larger system diagram: the storage reservoir is a porous medium where air occupies interconnected pore spaces rather than a large empty cavity.",
